\fs14\
\f1 \b0 \fs28 \pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 NSAssertionHandler objects are automatically created to handle false assertions. Assertion macros are used to evaluate a condition and, if the condition evaluates to false, the macros pass a string to an NSAssertionHandler object describing the failure. Each thread has its own NSAssertionHandler object. When invoked, an NSAssertionHandler prints an error message that includes the method and class (or function) containing the assertion, and raises an NSInternalInconsistencyException.\
\fs14\
\fs28 You use an assortment of macros to evaluate a condition\'d0these macros serve as a front end to NSAssertionHandler. These macros fall into two categories: those you use in Objective-C methods, and those you use in C functions. For example, \b NSAssert()\b0  is for use within methods and \b NSCAssert()\b0  is for use within functions. Each macro has two arguments: the condition, an expression that evaluates to true or false, and the NSString describing the failure. Other macros are available if more arguments are needed for a \b printf()\b0 -style description. For example, \b NSAssert1()\b0  is used within methods if an additional argument is needed as in:\
\fs14\
\pard \s19 \li1915 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx2413 \tx2889 \tx3421 \tx3953 \tx4457 \tx4933 \tx7005 \tx7509 \tx8013 \tx8517 \tx9021 \tx9525 \tx10029 \tx10533 \tx11037 \tx11513 \f2 \fs24 NSAssert1((0 <= component) && (component <= 255), \
\tab @"Value %i out of range!", component);\
\fs14\
\f1 \fs28 \pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 For more details on these macros see \b NSAssert()\b0 .\
\fs14\
\fs28 You create assertions only using the above macros\'d0you rarely need to invoke NSAssertionHandler methods directly. The macros for use inside methods and functions will send \b handleFailureInMethod:...\b0  and \b handleFailureInFunction:...\b0  to the current assertion handler respectively. The assertion handler for the current thread is obtained using the \b currentHandler\b0  class method.\
\fs14\
\fs28 Assertions are \i not\i0  compiled into code if the preprocessor macro NS_BLOCK_ASSERTIONS is defined.\
\fs14\
